In C++, variables are used to store data in the memory of the computer. Variables have different data types that determine the type of data they can store and the amount of memory they occupy. Here are some common data types in C++:
- int: used to store integers (whole numbers) such as 1, 2, -3, etc.
- float: used to store decimal numbers such as 1.2, 3.14, -0.5, etc.
- char: used to store single characters such as 'a', 'b', '1', etc.
- bool: used to store true or false values.
The scope of a variable determines where it can be accessed in the program. There are three types of scope in C++:
- Local: variables declared inside a function can only be accessed within that function.
- Global: variables declared outside any function can be accessed from any part of the program.
- Class: variables declared inside a class can be accessed by any member function of that class.
