One creative solution to display the current date and time on a webpage without using JavaScript is to use CSS animations and the :after pseudo-element.
First, create a div element with a unique class name, such as "clock". Then, use CSS to style the div element with a background color, border radius, and padding to create a circular clock face.
Next, use the :after pseudo-element to add the current time to the clock face. Set the content property to the current time using the attr() function, and use CSS to position and style the text.
To update the time every second, use CSS animations to rotate the :after pseudo-element. Set the animation duration to 60 seconds and use the steps() function to animate the rotation in one-second intervals.
Here is an example CSS code that achieves this:
.clock {
  width: 200px;
  height: 200px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background-color: #fff;
  padding: 20px;
  position: relative;
}
.clock:after {
  content: attr(data-time);
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  font-size: 24px;
  font-weight: bold;
  color: #000;
}
.clock:after {
  animation: rotate 60s linear infinite;
}
@keyframes rotate {
  0% {
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(0deg);
  }
  100% {
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(360deg);
  }
}
Finally, in the HTML code, add the current time to the data-time attribute of the clock div element using the time element and the datetime attribute:
<div class="clock" data-time="">
