Sure, here's a MATLAB code to calculate the roots of a quadratic equation with complex coefficients:
```
% Input coefficients a, b, and c
a = input('Enter coefficient a: ');
b = input('Enter coefficient b: ');
c = input('Enter coefficient c: ');
% Calculate discriminant
d = b^2 - 4*a*c;
% Calculate roots
if d > 0
    % Real and distinct roots
    x1 = (-b + sqrt(d))/(2*a);
    x2 = (-b - sqrt(d))/(2*a);
    fprintf('The roots are %.2f and %.2f\n', x1, x2);
elseif d == 0
    % Real and equal roots
    x = -b/(2*a);
    fprintf('The root is %.2f\n', x);
else
    % Complex roots
    realPart = -b/(2*a);
    imagPart = sqrt(-d)/(2*a);
    fprintf('The roots are %.2f + %.2fi and %.2f - %.2fi\n', realPart, imagPart, realPart, imagPart);
end
```
You can input the coefficients a, b, and c when prompted and the code will output the roots of the quadratic equation.

