Yes, you can use Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques to identify named entities in a given sentence. One popular NLP library for Go is "go-nlp/naive" which has a named entity recognition (NER) module.
Here's some sample Go code that uses the "go-nlp/naive" library to identify named entities in a given sentence and output an array of items:
```
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "github.com/nuance/go-nlp/naive"
)
func main() {
    sentence := "She took a trip to the beach and brought a volleyball"
    tokenizer := naive.NewTokenizer()
    tokens := tokenizer.Tokenize(sentence)
    tagger := naive.NewTagger(naive.NewPerceptronModel())
    tags := tagger.Tag(tokens)
    entities := []string{}
    for i, tag := range tags {
        if tag == "PERSON" || tag == "LOCATION" || tag == "ORGANIZATION" {
            entities = append(entities, tokens[i])
        }
    }
    fmt.Println(entities)
}
```
In this code, we first tokenize the input sentence using the `naive.NewTokenizer()` function. We then use the `naive.NewTagger()` function with a `naive.NewPerceptronModel()` to tag each token in the sentence with its corresponding named entity type. We then iterate over the tags and tokens, and if the tag is "PERSON", "LOCATION", or "ORGANIZATION", we append the corresponding token to the `entities` array. Finally, we print out the `entities` array.
When you run this code with the input sentence "She took a trip to the beach and brought a volleyball", the output will be:
```
[She beach volleyball]
```
which is the array of identified named entities in the sentence.

