To transfer the file in parts and optimize the transfer process, we can use a combination of Java's I/O classes and threading. Here's an example implementation:
```java
import java.io.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
public class FileTransfer {
    private static final int BUFFER_SIZE = 1024;
    private static final int PART_SIZE = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB
    private static final int MAX_THREADS = 4;
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        File sourceFile = new File("file.txt");
        File destFile = new File("/storage/emulated/0/file.txt");
        long fileSize = sourceFile.length();
        int numParts = (int) Math.ceil((double) fileSize / PART_SIZE);
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(MAX_THREADS);
        CompletionService<Integer> completionService = new ExecutorCompletionService<>(executor);
        try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(sourceFile);
             FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream(destFile)) {
            for (int i = 0; i < numParts; i++) {
                int partSize = (i == numParts - 1) ? (int) (fileSize % PART_SIZE) : PART_SIZE;
                byte[] buffer = new byte[partSize];
                fis.read(buffer);
                int finalI = i;
                Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
                    fos.seek(finalI * PART_SIZE);
                    fos.write(buffer);
                    return finalI;
                };
                completionService.submit(task);
            }
            for (int i = 0; i < numParts; i++) {
                Future<Integer> future = completionService.take();
                int partNumber = future.get();
                System.out.println("Part " + partNumber + " transferred successfully");
            }
        } catch (IOException | InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            executor.shutdown();
        }
    }
}
```
In this implementation, we divide the file into parts of 1 MB each and use a fixed thread pool to transfer each part in parallel. We use a CompletionService to keep track of the completion of each task and print a message when each part is transferred successfully.
To measure the transfer speed, we can add timing code to the task and print the transfer rate at the end of the program. Here's an updated version of the task:
```java
Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
    long startTime = System.nanoTime();
    fos.seek(finalI * PART_SIZE);
    fos.write(buffer);
    long endTime = System.nanoTime();
    long elapsedTime = endTime - startTime;
    double transferRate = (double) partSize / elapsedTime * 1e9;
    System.out.printf("Part %d transferred in %.2f seconds at %.2f MB/s%n",
            finalI, elapsedTime / 1e9, transferRate / 1e6);
    return finalI;
};
``` 
This code measures the transfer time and calculates the transfer rate in MB/s. We can use this information to optimize the transfer process by adjusting the part size and the number of threads to achieve the best transfer rate.

Euler's Formula, also known as Euler's Identity, states that e^(ix) = cos(x) + i*sin(x), where e is the mathematical constant approximately equal to 2.71828, i is the imaginary unit equal to the square root of -1, and x is any real number. This formula relates the exponential function, trigonometric functions, and complex numbers in a fundamental way. 
One way to prove Euler's Formula is by using the Taylor series expansion of e^(ix), sin(x), and cos(x). Another proof involves using complex analysis and the concept of power series. Regardless of the proof, Euler's Formula has wide-ranging applications in various fields such as physics, engineering, and computer science. 
In physics, Euler's Formula is used to describe the behavior of waves, such as electromagnetic waves and sound waves. In engineering, it is used in signal processing and control systems. In computer science, it is used in digital signal processing, image processing, and cryptography. 
When comparing and contrasting Euler's Formula with other mathematical formulas, it is important to note that each formula has its own unique properties and applications. The Pythagorean Theorem relates to the sides of a right triangle, while the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus relates to the concepts of differentiation and integration. Euler's Formula, on the other hand, relates to the exponential function, trigonometric functions, and complex numbers. 
In terms of code implementation, here are examples of how Euler's Formula can be written in Python, Java, and C++:
Python:
```
import cmath
import math
x = math.pi/4
euler = cmath.exp(x*1j)
cos_x = euler.real
sin_x = euler.imag
```
Java:
```
import java.lang.Math;
double x = Math.PI/4;
