A quadratic function is a polynomial of degree two. It has the form:
\\begin{equation*}
f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c
\\end{equation*}
where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants and $a\\neq0$. 
\\subsection*{Vertex Form}
The vertex form of a quadratic function is:
\\begin{equation*}
f(x) = a(x-h)^2 + k
\\end{equation*}
where $(h,k)$ is the vertex of the parabola.
\\subsection*{Factored Form}
The factored form of a quadratic function is:
\\begin{equation*}
f(x) = a(x-r_1)(x-r_2)
\\end{equation*}
where $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the roots of the quadratic equation.
\\subsection*{Standard Form}
The standard form of a quadratic function is:
\\begin{equation*}
f(x) = ax^2 + bx + c
\\end{equation*}
where $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants.
\\end{document}
